logic.py,src/logic.py,64728a632ee41b34db530d1c75b45b295b9915da,ddahan/cve-explainer,https://api.github.com/repositories/953103362/contents/src/logic.py?ref=268c9892ce774891bdae8eb76e3705ae84fa7498,https://api.github.com/repositories/953103362/git/blobs/64728a632ee41b34db530d1c75b45b295b9915da,https://github.com/ddahan/cve-explainer/blob/268c9892ce774891bdae8eb76e3705ae84fa7498/src/logic.py,A CVE explainer tool in CLI,,0,0,0,5,2025-03-22T16:09:34Z,0,0,"# CVE Explainer

This project is a CLI tool able to:
  - summarize a CVE, by first fetching its structured data on NIST NVD API, and then calling a LLM (local or remote) to generate this summary
  - scan the given `poetry.lock` files locally, to detect vulnerabilities against those packages.

You can choose between local LLM (`llama3.2`) or remote API call to OpenAI (`gpt-4o`) to generate the explanation. Note that ChatGPT is way faster (a few seconds) than Llama (a few minutes, depending on your machine) to generate an answer, but it requires an API key with a paid account.

## Installation

### Method 1 (recommended): Automatic installation with devcontainers
- Install [Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/)
- Install VS Code editor with [Dev Containers extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-vscode-remote.remote-containers)
- Run command: `clone repository in container volume` and enter this repository public Github URL. The project will be automatically downloaded and installed with defined extensions, in an isolated Docker container.
- Create a `.env` file, using `.env.example` as an example.

### Method 2: Manual installation
- Download and install [ollama](https://ollama.com/)
- Create a Python 3.12.9 environment using [Poetry](https://python-poetry.org/) package manager
- Run `poetry install` to install all Python packages
- Set `PYTHONPATH` env var to `src/` directory so pytest can discover modules
- Create a `.env` file, using `.env.example` as an example.

## Usage

- If you aim to use local LLM:
  - run `ollama serve` in a terminal to run a server
  - run `ollama pull llama3.2` to download this model locally (2.0 GB are necessary)
- If you prefer to use ChatGPT API instead, please ensure `OPENAI_API_KEY` value is set in the `.env` file.


```sh
python -m cve_explainer --help
```

will give you a detailed and up-to-date description of how to use the command, with all parameters and their description. Please use this command first to have a usage overview.

### Example

This example command:

```
> python -m cve_explainer CVE-2023-41105 --model gpt-4o
```

will output something like:

```
$ python -m cve_explainer CVE-2023-41105 --model gpt-4o
2025-03-22 15:01:28.978 | INFO     | __main__:explain:58 - CVE-2023-41105 details have been fetched successfully.
2025-03-22 15:01:28.978 | INFO     | __main__:explain:59 - Generating an explanation using gpt-4o LLM ...
================================== Ai Message ==================================

There is a security flaw identified in versions Python 3.11 up to 3.11.4. It's been found that if you use certain characters (specifically ""\0"", meaning a null byte) in a file path, a function that tries to simplify or normalize the path might unexpectedly cut off your path at these points. As a result, a filename that might have been blocked or considered unsafe in older versions of Python (like 3.10.x) could suddenly pass through without issue in Python 3.11.x. 

This issue is deemed serious and could potentially be exploited over a network, leading to integrity problems — this means unauthorized changes to data could happen, but it won’t necessarily affect confidentiality (like stealing data) or service availability (like denying services). Therefore, its potential impact is considered high. [...]

2025-03-22 15:01:34.075 | INFO     | __main__:explain:61 - None
2025-03-22 15:01:34.473 | INFO     | __main__:explain:69 - 71 packages have been found in ../cve-explainer/poetry.lock.
2025-03-22 15:01:34.474 | INFO     | __main__:explain:71 - Analyzing these 71 packages against CVE-2023-41105 ...
2025-03-22 15:01:34.474 | SUCCESS  | __main__:explain:81 - Congratulations, no vulnerability has been found!
```

## Run tests

```sh
pytest
```

> NOTE: tests can be run directly in the VS Code GUI too.

##  Limitations
- A vulnerability will be considered effective no matter the actual package usage in the codebase
- Only projects containing `poetry.lock` files can be scanned (parsing `requirements.txt` is more complex)
- Only local packages can be scanned

## Technical Characteristics

- Built with Python 3.12 and [Poetry](https://github.com/python-poetry/poetry) package manager
- Fully typed in strict mode, using [Pylance](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-python.vscode-pylance)
- Linting, formatting and import sorting are handled by [Ruff](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff)
- CLI tooling is handled by [Typer](https://typer.tiangolo.com/). This ensures a better experience than manual arg parsing. It uses [Rich](https://github.com/Textualize/rich) behind the scenes for a clean terminal display
- Uses [Langchain](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langchain) as an abstraction to work with multiple LLMs using the same API
- Uses [Loguru](https://github.com/Delgan/loguru) for very simple logging
- Environment settings are provided using [Pydantic Settings](https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/pydantic_settings/)
- Tests are written with [pytest](https://github.com/pytest-dev/pytest)
- [Nist NVD public API](https://nvd.nist.gov/) is used to retrieve detailed information about CVEs


## Next steps

- [ ] Check if a package like `pip-audit` could make the whole process simpler/stronger
- [ ] Add the ability to scan a public Github repository in addition to local folders
- [ ] Check vulnerabilities against package usage rather than package setup
- [ ] Handle other languages, or other packages managers that use requirements.txt files
- [ ] Add troubleshooting steps and more detailed ""manual installation"" in README
- [ ] Propose a configuration which is less coupled with VS Code (editor.config, mypy, etc.)
- [ ] Add more tests, especially on functions which call external APIs (using mocks)
- [ ] Add streaming features to generate LLM result progressively (especially for local LLM)."
RVS.py,RVS.py,81f66e8861764d0f8f4e47f08217a22cd022df27,ssm123ssm/docGPT-pharm,https://api.github.com/repositories/672128468/contents/RVS.py?ref=555b8517ac4fbe0e9caf8e9ec97caf93a1b678ae,https://api.github.com/repositories/672128468/git/blobs/81f66e8861764d0f8f4e47f08217a22cd022df27,https://github.com/ssm123ssm/docGPT-pharm/blob/555b8517ac4fbe0e9caf8e9ec97caf93a1b678ae/RVS.py,,,0,0,0,10,2023-08-03T08:35:30Z,10,2,"# docGPT - Document Intelligence 

Welcome to **docGPT**, an easily implemented document intelligence program written as an abstraction over langchain. Simply put all the unstructured data (eg PDF files) in the `data` folder and start asking questions about them!

Useful for Retrieval and Summarization tasks.

## Overview

Place your files in the `data` folder. Install dependencies and run

```
from docGPT_core import * 
from RVS import *

set_tokens(OPENAI_TOKEN='OPENAI TOKEN HERE',HF_TOKEN=""HF TOKEN HERE"")
llm  =  Llm(model_type='gpt-3.5-turbo')
openai_embeddings  =  Embedding(model_type='openai')
persona  =  Persona(personality_type='explainer')

vs = VectorStore(embedding_model=openai_embeddings)
chain = Chain(retriever=vs, llm=llm, persona=persona)
```

You can start chatting with your data now.

`chain.qa(inputs={""query"": ""Your question""})`

## Installation

Clone this repo. Install dependencies langchain, gradio, numpy and sklearn using `pip install -r requirements.txt`

## Preparation

Import docGPT_core and RVS modules.

```
from docGPT_core import * 
from RVS import *
```
You have to define the OpenAI token, the LLM to be used and the embedding model to be used. The default LLM is OpenAI `gpt-3.5-turbo` and default embedding model is `text-embedding-ada-002`. If you are planning on using Huggingface models, HugingFace token also needs to be set.

```
#setting tokens
set_tokens(OPENAI_TOKEN='OPENAI TOKEN HERE',HF_TOKEN=""HF TOKEN HERE"")

#setting models
llm  =  Llm(model_type='gpt-3.5-turbo')
openai_embeddings  =  Embedding(model_type='openai')

#setting the personality of the model
persona  =  Persona(personality_type='explainer')
```

 ## Vectorstore creation

Place all the files you want to create the vectorstore for, in the `data` folder and run call the VectorStore function. You can pass the chunk size  and the overlaps if necessary. 

```
vs = VectorStore(embedding_model=openai_embeddings)
```

This might take some time depending on the size of the documents. If the data in the `data` folder need OCR, additional packages including Tesseract OCR needs to be installed.  

You can save the created vectorstore in disk to avoid re-creating it with `vs.save('my_vectorstore.pkl')` and reload it later with `load_vectorstore('my_vectorstore.pkl')`

## Chain for prompting

The QA chain can be initiated with one line of code

`chain = Chain(retriever=vs, llm=llm, persona=persona)`, 

The `retriever` used in the chain object creation in above example is the default retriever attached to the vectorstore. If you want to retrieve n-number of documents, not the default 3 documents, use Retriever class.

Eg: `chain = Chain(retriever=Retriever(vs, k=4), llm=llm, persona=persona)`

and can query the vectorstore with 

`chain.qa(inputs={""query"": ""Your question""})`

## Summarization

RVS module provides functions for summarization.

`summarize()` function returns the summary of the vectorstore as a passage or as key points.

eg:  `summarize(vectorstore=vs, llm=llm, max_tokens=2500, summary=False, keypoints=True)`

Toggle `summary` and `keypoints` booleans to return either kind of summary. The method uses Representative Vector Summarization method explained in [https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.00479v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.00479v1).
Change the `max_tokens` parameter, depending on the number of chunks to be included and the size of each chunk. Suggestion is to start with a value like 2000, and depending on the answer, fine tune.

### Further control

Mentioned above is the highest level API for creating the QA engine. Please refer this document for finer control.
